  "lessons": [
    {
      "key": "Pre-Assessment",
      "name": "Pre-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 1,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "assessment": true
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Pre-Assessment",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-2",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 1: Investigating AI Tools",
      "name": "Lesson 1: Prompting AI",
      "absolute_position": 2,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 1,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do you get AI to do exactly what you want?**\n\nIn this introductory lesson, students transition from passive users to critical evaluators of AI. By systematically testing an AI image generator, students move beyond \"gut feelings\" to evidence-based judgments. They explore the high-level mechanics of how to write a good prompt.",
        "student_overview": "**How do you get AI to do exactly what you want?**\n\nIs AI a magic tool, or just a new way to find patterns? In this lesson, you’ll become an AI investigator. You will systematically test an AI image generator to see how small changes in your instructions lead to different results."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 1: Investigating AI Tools",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 2: A Deeper Look Into AI",
      "name": "Lesson 2: Project Launch",
      "absolute_position": 3,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 2,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do patterns in data shape what an AI generates?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore how AI systems actually function. Through an unplugged simulation, students learn how machine learning models rely on human-provided data and patterns to make probabilistic predictions. By acting as both \"Data\" and \"AI,\" students recognize that AI logic is not \"magic\" but a result of human choices and dataset limitations.\n\nThis lesson also launches the unit’s capstone project: the Responsible AI Use Guide. Students adopt the professional persona of \"Testers\"—similar to crash test engineers or game QA—to frame their role in evaluating AI accuracy and safety. This positioning encourages students to evaluate AI outputs critically rather than accepting them at face value.",
        "preparation": "- Print and cut out the Secret Patterns",
        "student_overview": "**How do patterns in data shape what an AI generates?**\n\nIn this lesson, you’ll go behind the scenes of AI to figure out how it really works. Last time, it may have felt like AI was doing something magical—but now you’ll step into the role of an engineer to see what’s actually happening. Through a hands-on simulation, you’ll act as both the “Data” and the “AI,” creating patterns, training a partner with examples, and making predictions based only on the information provided. You’ll discover that AI doesn’t have a brain or feelings—it looks for patterns in data and makes its best guess. You’ll also see how the results depend on the data and rules humans choose, which means AI can sometimes make mistakes or follow incomplete logic.\n\nThis lesson also launches your unit project: the **Responsible AI Use Guide**. You’ll take on the role of a professional “Tester”—like a crash test engineer or game quality tester—whose job is to check how safe, accurate, and helpful an AI tool really is. Instead of just using AI, you’ll learn how to evaluate it thoughtfully and responsibly. By the end of the lesson, you’ll understand that AI outputs aren’t magic—they’re predictions shaped by patterns, data, and human decisions."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 2: A Deeper Look Into AI",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 3: Responsible Co-Creation",
      "name": "Lesson 3: AI-Assisted Creation",
      "absolute_position": 4,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 3,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How might we balance human creativity with AI power to create responsibly?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the concept of co-creation by navigating the relationship between human agency and AI-generated output. Using the metaphor of a Pilot and Co-Pilot, students analyze when to trust AI’s pattern recognition and when to remain in command based on human training and instinct. Through hands-on explorations in AI Chat and Sketch Lab, students learn to articulate ethical boundaries for their creative work. The lesson concludes with students evaluating the sociotechnical trade-offs of AI, including environmental impacts and artist rights, to establish personal \"Co-Pilot Guidelines\" for responsible use.",
        "preparation": "- Print multiple copies of each Exploration Guide and set up as stations around the room",
        "student_overview": "**How might we balance human creativity with AI power to create responsibly?**\n\nBecome the Pilot in Command of your own creative projects. You will work with an AI \"Co-Pilot\" to explore how humans and machines can work together. This is a process called co-creation. You’ll test AI tools to help write stories, build campaigns, or grow a video channel while learning how to protect your unique voice. Finally, you will investigate the real-world impact of AI on society to decide exactly where you will draw the line between helpful assistance and losing creative control."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 3: Responsible Co-Creation",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 5: Trust and Credibility",
      "name": "Lesson 4: AI-Generated Images",
      "absolute_position": 5,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 4,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do we use AI responsibly when it generates convincing but incomplete information?**\n\nIn this lesson, students work in groups to explore how AI generates outputs that appear credible but may contain gaps, assumptions, or missing context. Through repeated testing within a chosen mini-exploration, students identify the patterns that create a sense of believability and analyze where those outputs break down under scrutiny. The lesson builds students’ capacity to pause, verify, and critically evaluate AI-generated information before trusting it.",
        "preparation": "- Print multiple copies of each Exploration Guide and set up as stations around the room",
        "student_overview": "**How do we use AI responsibly when it generates convincing but incomplete information?**\n\nToday you’ll investigate how AI can create responses that look and sound convincing — even when something is missing. In groups, you’ll choose a mini exploration and test AI outputs to see what makes them feel believable and where they break down. Your goal is to figure out how to slow down, check details, and decide what’s actually trustworthy."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 5: Trust and Credibility",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 4: Guidelines for Working with AI",
      "name": "Lesson 5: Creating a Chatbot",
      "absolute_position": 6,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 5,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can we establish clear boundaries to ensure AI remains a tool for support rather than a replacement for human creativity?**\n\nIn this lesson, students transition from experimentation to synthesis by establishing a personal and collective \"Co-Pilot’s Code.\" Having previously explored AI’s impact on originality and authenticity, students now work in \"Expert Groups\" to identify the \"Tipping Point\"—the moment where AI assistance begins to overshadow human voice and values. Through a Jigsaw synthesis, students share findings across different creative domains to identify universal patterns that arise when co-creating with AI. The lesson culminates in the drafting the \"Co-Creation Guidelines\" section of the unit project.",
        "student_overview": "**How can we establish clear boundaries to ensure AI remains a tool for support rather than a replacement for human creativity?**\n\nIs AI the \"Pilot\" of your work, or are you? In this lesson, you will look back at your experiments with AI to find the \"Tipping Point\"—that specific moment where a project stops feeling like yours and starts feeling \"mostly AI.” Using evidence from your explorations, mini-lessons, and conversations, you will begin drafting the \"Co-Creation Guidelines\" portion of your unit project."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 4: Guidelines for Working with AI",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 6: The AI Credibility Guide",
      "name": "Lesson 6: Getting Feedback",
      "absolute_position": 7,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 6,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How do you decide when to trust AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, students synthesize their investigations into AI-generated outputs by creating a Trust Meter as part of their responsible AI guide. Rather than evaluating a single output, students generalize patterns they observed to define levels of trust and identify user moves that increase responsible use. The focus is on transferring analysis skills into practical decision-making guidance.",
        "student_overview": "**How do you decide when to trust AI?**\n\nToday you’ll turn what you’ve learned about AI into advice for others. You’ll design a Trust Meter to help people decide when to trust AI and when to slow down and check. Your goal is to create a clear, helpful guide for using AI responsibly."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 6: The AI Credibility Guide",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 7: Exploring Data in AI",
      "name": "Lesson 7: Verifying AI Output",
      "absolute_position": 8,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 7,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How does the data we share affect our safety and privacy?**\n\nIn this lesson, students explore the subtle boundary between public sharing and private discovery by analyzing how AI systems synthesize invisible patterns from everyday data to create highly personal profiles. Using several explorations, students learn to categorize information as Public, Private, or Sensitive. They experiment with AI-assisted anonymization and pattern recognition, developing the literacy and critical thinking needed to understand how small, public details can be transformed into sensitive insights, ultimately weighing the trade-offs of digital privacy in an AI-enabled world.",
        "student_overview": "**How does the data we share affect our safety and privacy?**\n\nIn this lesson, you will become a digital detective to uncover how AI systems find invisible patterns in the things we share online, like how a single photo can reveal your location or daily routine. You’ll explore ways to categorize data as public, private, and sensitive, and explore how to protect your privacy while using AI tools."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 7: Exploring Data in AI",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 8: Defending Your Data",
      "name": "Lesson 8: AI Privacy and Safety",
      "absolute_position": 9,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 8,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**What are recommendations and best practices for sharing data with AI?**\n\nIn this lesson, students transition from guided investigations of AI data privacy to independent synthesis. They begin to develop nuanced, evidence-based security strategies. By analyzing patterns across different data types—including shopping habits, public profiles, and social media—students evaluate how AI systems infer sensitive personal information from seemingly mundane digital footprints. This lesson centers on the sociotechnical reality of modern privacy, tasking students with drafting actionable Safety and Privacy guidelines for their final projects that prioritize human agency and critical skepticism.",
        "student_overview": "**What are recommendations and best practices for sharing data with AI?**\n\nCan you stay safe online without giving up technology entirely? In this lesson, you will become a data expert by comparing what you’ve learned about how AI uses your personal information—from the things you buy to the things you post. You will look for \"Red Flags\" and patterns in how AI makes guesses about you, then use that evidence to build your own personal \"Privacy Shield.\" By the end of the lesson, you will have a set of realistic, evidence-based rules to help you and your community navigate an AI-powered world safely."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 8: Defending Your Data",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 9: Refining Our Product",
      "name": "Lesson 9: Own Your Guide",
      "absolute_position": 10,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 9,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How can I incorporate both human and AI feedback to strengthen my product?**\n\nIn this lesson, students transition from creators to critics as they refine their AI Guides. Students will engage with both an AI chatbot as well as their peers, receiving feedback from both. By navigating these two distinct types of feedback, students learn to identify the unique strengths of human judgment—such as understanding social context and relatability—while leveraging AI to uncover logical gaps and missing evidence. The lesson concludes with students using generative AI as a tool to create images in order to visually communicate their guidance and advice.",
        "student_overview": "**How can I incorporate both human and AI feedback to strengthen my product?**\n\nHow do you know if your work is actually ready for the real world? In this lesson, you’ll put your AI Guide through a \"stress test\" by facing off against AI chatbots designed to examine your logic. You will then shift gears to work with your classmates, discovering the human understanding that AI might often miss. Finally, you’ll create images in order to visually communicate your guidance and advice to your audience."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 9: Refining Our Product",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Lesson 10: The Final Verdict",
      "name": "Lesson 10: The Final Verdict",
      "absolute_position": 11,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 10,
      "properties": {
        "overview": "**How should humans interact with AI systems?**\n\nIn this culminating lesson, students synthesize their learning from the unit by participating in a structured debate about how humans should interact with AI systems. Using evidence from prior investigations, they construct and defend a claim, evaluate the strength of different arguments, and deliberate toward a final verdict. The lesson emphasizes evidence-based reasoning, human oversight, and reflection on how students’ understanding of AI’s capabilities, limitations, and risks has evolved.",
        "student_overview": "**How should humans interact with AI systems?**\n\nIn this lesson, you will decide how humans should use AI and defend your thinking with evidence from our investigations. You will listen to other arguments, question the strength of the evidence, and work toward a final verdict. You will also reflect on how your ideas about AI have changed during this unit."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "Lesson 10: The Final Verdict",
        "lesson_group.key": "lessonGroup-3",
        "script.name": "thinking-critically-about-ai-2026"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "Post-Assessment",
      "name": "Post-Assessment",
      "absolute_position": 12,
      "lockable": true,
      "has_lesson_plan": false,
